extends RefCounted
## The final Help chapter: player-facing rules, not implementation notes.
## Verified against engine/combat.gd, the effect classes, fem/_rules.gd and
## Wizards' Comprehensive Rules (magic.wizards.com/en/rules), especially
## combat, keyword abilities, costs, counters and delayed triggers.
## Keep examples textual so this reference also works with packs disabled.

static func portal_intro() -> Dictionary:
	return _page("Portal — A first step into Magic", [
			["Original Portal, 1997", "Pack 6 adds the original Portal set: straightforward creatures, spells and beautiful basic lands. Enable it in Options → Card Packs, then choose Portal in Deck Builder → Extras. Turn the other sources off to browse its 200 different cards and 215 printings. Select a card, then use the Card variant stone medallion just below the large preview's lower-right corner to choose artwork, including four versions of each basic land. Your saved deck remembers the choice."],
			["Read the current rules", "The original pictures preserve their printed wording. Gameplay uses current card types and rules. Some cards printed as sorceries are now instants. Assassin's Blade, Deep Wood and similar tricks can be cast only after an opponent attacks you, during Declare Attackers — before blockers are declared."],
			["More lands and fewer blockers", "Summer Bloom allows three additional land plays this turn. Charging Rhino and Stalking Tiger cannot be blocked by more than one creature. If such a creature also requires two or more blockers, it cannot be blocked."],
			["Last Chance", "Last Chance grants an extra turn, but you lose at the beginning of that turn's end step. The most recently created extra turn happens first. A normal Time Walk turn does not inherit Last Chance's loss condition."],
			["The large mana symbols", "Portal basic-land previews retain the original printed card and its large mana symbol. Animated lands show the live game frame so their creature statistics remain visible. Saved decks remember one chosen printing per card name."],
		])


static func pages() -> Array:
	return [
		_page("Abilities — Combat", [
			["Flying and reach", "A flying attacker can be blocked only by creatures with flying or reach. Reach does not let a creature fly. Giving flying after blockers are chosen does not remove an existing block."],
			["First strike", "First strikers deal combat damage before creatures without first strike. A creature killed by that early damage does not strike back. First strike does not double the damage."],
			["Trample", "An attacker can assign excess combat damage to the defending player after assigning lethal damage to its blockers. Without trample, an attacker that became blocked normally stays blocked even if its blockers leave."],
			["Vigilance, haste and defender", "Vigilance means attacking does not tap the creature. Haste removes the usual summoning-sickness restriction on attacking and tap-symbol abilities. Defender prevents attacking unless another effect specifically allows it."],
			["Landwalk and menace", "Landwalk makes an attacker unblockable while the defender controls the named land type. Menace means it cannot be blocked by just one creature: the defender needs at least two legal blockers. Goblin War Drums gives this blocking restriction to your creatures."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Banding and protection", [
			["Banding when attacking", "You may group creatures with banding and up to one creature without banding into an attacking band. If a creature blocks a member, the whole band becomes blocked. Band members do not share flying, trample or other abilities."],
			["Banding changes damage assignment", "When an attacking band includes banding, its controller chooses how its blockers assign damage among that band. A defending creature with banding similarly lets its controller assign the blocked attacker's damage. Banding does not merge the creatures or their toughness."],
			["Protection", "Protection from a quality prevents damage from matching sources, stops matching Auras from enchanting the creature, stops matching creatures from blocking it, and stops matching spells or abilities from targeting it. Protection from black does not stop every black effect: a non-targeting destroy-all effect can still destroy it."],
			["Shroud", "A permanent with shroud cannot be targeted by either player's spells or abilities, including your own. It can still be affected by effects that do not target. Deep Spawn and Homarid Warrior can gain temporary shroud, but their abilities also tap them and delay their next untap."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Costs, triggers and survival", [
			["Activated abilities: cost : effect", "Pay everything before the colon when you activate: mana, tapping, sacrifices, discards or counter removal. Those costs are not returned if the ability is countered or loses its target. A tap-symbol cost needs an untapped source; a summoning-sick creature cannot pay it without haste."],
			["Triggered and static abilities", "When, whenever and at usually introduce triggered abilities. They wait on the Spell Chain and can be answered. Static abilities apply continuously while their conditions hold, without an activation. Mana abilities normally produce mana immediately rather than waiting on the chain."],
			["Regeneration", "Regeneration replaces destruction: tap the creature, remove its marked damage and remove it from combat. It does not return a dead creature. With modern damage timing, create the shield before lethal damage; with classic damage timing, use the regeneration window. Regeneration cannot stop sacrifice, exile, zero toughness or an effect that forbids regeneration."],
			["Counters and temporary boosts", "Counters stay on a permanent until removed or until it leaves the battlefield. A +1/+1 counter changes both stats; +1/+0 and +0/+1 counters change only one. An until-end-of-turn boost expires at cleanup instead. Damage is not a counter and does not reduce the displayed toughness."],
			["Tokens and creature types", "A token is a permanent and follows its creature's normal rules, including summoning sickness. A token that leaves the battlefield cannot later return. Creature types such as Thrull, Goblin, Merfolk and Fungus matter only when a card refers to them; sharing a type does not give creatures shared abilities."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Fallen Empires resources", [
			["Spore counters", "Thallids and related creatures gain spore counters during your upkeep. Remove three to pay for the printed ability: making a Saproling, dealing damage, preventing combat damage or regenerating. Spore counters alone do not increase power or toughness. Fungal Bloom adds a spore counter to a Fungus."],
			["Storage lands", "These lands enter tapped. You may leave one tapped during your untap step, and it gains a storage counter during your upkeep if it is tapped. Once untapped, use its mana ability and remove the chosen number of storage counters to produce that much mana."],
			["Mana conversion and High Tide", "Initiates of the Ebon Hand and Farrelite Priest turn one mana into the printed color without tapping. Using one at least four times in a turn creates a sacrifice trigger for the next end step. Implements of Sacrifice costs one mana, tapping and sacrificing itself to make two mana of one color. High Tide adds blue mana when an Island is tapped for mana that turn."],
			["Tide, net, time and credit counters", "Homarid and Tidal Influence change their effects as tide counters rise, then reset. Merseine's net counters keep its host from untapping; the host's controller may pay to remove them. Tourach's Gate consumes time counters at upkeep. Icatian Moneychanger stores credit counters that its sacrifice ability turns into life."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Fallen Empires combat tricks", [
			["Sacrifices and crew taps", "Goblin Warrens sacrifices two Goblins to make three. Night Soil exiles two creature cards from one graveyard to make a Saproling. War Machine and Hand of Justice require other creatures to tap as costs. A cost that says to tap an eligible creature is different from that creature paying its own tap symbol, so newly arrived helpers can be used."],
			["Unblocked-attack abilities", "Farrel's Zealot, Farrel's Mantle, Mindstab Thrull and Necrite trigger after blockers are declared if their attacker is unblocked. Delif's artifacts first set up a delayed trigger. Read whether the card replaces combat-damage assignment, prevents damage, or sacrifices the attacker: these are different effects."],
			["Effects that last while a condition holds", "Seasinger's control lasts while it remains tapped and under your control; Thrull Champion also needs you to keep control of the Champion. Spirit Shield and Zelyon Sword maintain their bonuses while tapped. Untapping or losing the required control ends the effect; tapping or taking the source back does not restart it."],
			["Coin flips and delayed effects", "Goblin Kites grants flying now, then flips a coin at the next end step; losing means sacrificing that creature if you still control it. Orcish Captain flips for a bonus or penalty. These are genuine random outcomes. Rainbow Vale gives its mana now and changes control through a later end-step trigger."],
			["New objects, not returned effects", "A card that leaves the battlefield and returns is a new permanent. Old counters, temporary effects and abilities waiting to affect its previous incarnation do not automatically follow it back. An ability already on the Spell Chain can still resolve even if its source leaves."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Ice Age resources", [
			["Snow lands", "Snow is a supertype, not a sixth mana color. Snow-Covered Island is still a basic Island and makes blue mana. A card counts snow lands only when its instructions say so. Snow landwalk checks the defending player's snow land of the named type."],
			["Cumulative upkeep", "At the beginning of your upkeep, put an age counter on that permanent. Then pay its upkeep cost once for each age counter, or sacrifice it. The whole payment is optional but must be paid in full. Multiple cumulative-upkeep abilities trigger separately and use the same age counters. Sacrifice cannot be regenerated."],
			["Restricted mana", "Adarkar Unicorn and Snowfall can produce mana usable only for cumulative upkeep. Soldevi Machinist's mana is only for activated abilities of artifacts, not for casting artifact spells. The game keeps restricted mana separate and checks what you are paying for."],
			["Delayed card draws", "Many Ice Age spells draw at the beginning of the next turn's upkeep, not immediately. The draw is already scheduled even if its source leaves play. If every target of the original spell becomes illegal, that spell does not resolve and does not schedule its draw."],
			["Wind counters and one-time abilities", "Freyalise's Winds adds a counter when a permanent becomes tapped. During its controller's untap step, removing all wind counters replaces untapping it. Other untap effects still work normally. Goblin Ski Patrol says activate only once: countering that activation does not restore the use. Leaving and returning creates a new object."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Ice Age zones and control", [
			["Abilities in the graveyard", "Most abilities work only on the battlefield. Ashen Ghoul and Whiteout explicitly let you activate from your graveyard; their timing and costs still apply. A graveyard ability on the Spell Chain does not give every other card in that graveyard permission to act."],
			["Playing cards from exile", "Exile does not normally let you play a card. Elkin Bottle and Ice Cauldron give permission for the particular exiled card. Normal costs and timing still apply, including the land-per-turn limit. Face-down exiled cards stay hidden unless an effect lets a player look at them."],
			["Storing mana", "Jeweled Amulet remembers the mana type actually paid to charge it. Ice Cauldron remembers the amounts and types paid and releases mana usable only for its last exiled card. Removing the artifact does not cancel the permission to cast a card already exiled with it."],
			["Borrowing and holding permanents", "Ray of Command and Magus of the Unseen borrow until end of turn and later tap the returned permanent through a trigger. Merieke's control ends when you lose control of Merieke, but her destruction trigger waits until she untaps or leaves. A newer control effect can override an older one; ending it reveals any older effect that is still valid."],
			["Claims on dead creatures", "Krovikan Vampire checks at each end step for creatures it damaged that died that turn. Seraph first triggers when a damaged creature dies, then schedules the return. The card must remain in that graveyard. Leaving and returning makes a new object: old damage and old claims do not follow a fresh incarnation."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Ice Age combat and adaptations", [
			["Attacking and blocking costs", "Orcish Conscripts needs at least two other attacking creatures to attack, or two other blocking creatures to block. Errantry's host can attack only alone. Flooded Woodlands and Reclamation charge land sacrifices to attack with the named color; you may decline to attack instead. Hipparion charges mana to block a creature with power 3 or greater."],
			["Melee and General Jarkeld", "Melee lets the attacker choose the defender's blocks for that combat; the defender still decides whether to pay any blocking costs. Unblocked attackers are untapped and removed from combat through triggered abilities. General Jarkeld exchanges the blockers of two blocked attackers only if the new blocks are legal."],
			["Gaze of Pain", "Each unblocked attacker gets a targeted trigger. You may have that creature deal damage equal to its power to the target creature; if you do, it assigns no combat damage that turn. This is not combat damage, and a creature that was blocked does not qualify just because its blocker later leaves."],
			["Drought and Soul Burn", "Drought adds Swamp sacrifices for each printed black mana symbol in a spell's cost or an ability's activation cost. Soul Burn's X is not extra black symbols: it accepts only actual black or red mana. Cost reductions can reduce what you pay for X without reducing X's damage; life gain counts only black mana actually spent on X and is capped by the damage and the victim's life or toughness."],
			["Documented digital adaptations", "Balduvian Shaman changes a color only on one of the five colored Circles of Protection you control; it cannot rewrite arbitrary enchantment text. It still grants cumulative upkeep. Game of Chaos stops after 30 flips in one resolution. These differences are also printed on the digital cards."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Homelands resources and locks", [
			["Finite counters", "Serrated Arrows starts with three arrowheads; removing one pays for a -1/-1 counter on a creature. At your upkeep, an empty Arrows is sacrificed. Clockwork Steed and Swarm lose a +1/+0 counter at end of combat after participating; their X-and-tap ability recharges only during your upkeep, to at most four counters."],
			["Giant Oyster", "Target a tapped creature. While the Oyster remains tapped, that creature skips normal untapping and receives a -1/-1 counter in each of your draw steps. Untapping or removing the Oyster ends the lock and triggers removal of all -1/-1 counters on that creature. Tapping the Oyster again does not restart the old effect."],
			["Putting cards into play", "Didgeridoo and Willow Priestess put eligible cards from your hand directly onto the battlefield. This is not casting a spell, so Aether Storm does not stop it. Ordinary entry triggers and summoning sickness still apply. Any player may pay four life to activate Aether Storm's destruction ability."],
			["Taxes and untap restrictions", "Koskun Falls charges two mana for each creature attacking its controller. Paying for one attacker does not pay for the rest. Some cards say your next untap step, while others refer to the affected creature's next normal untap. Changing control does not turn those phrases into the same instruction."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Homelands combat and ownership", [
			["Damage redirection", "Hazduhr the Abbot redirects up to the chosen X damage from a white creature you control to himself. Daughter of Autumn redirects the next one damage from any target white creature. Damage keeps its original source and combat status. Protection and prevention still apply to its new recipient; redirection is not a toughness bonus."],
			["Shroud permission", "Autumn Willow has shroud. Her green-mana ability gives the chosen player permission to target her for that turn; it does not remove shroud for everyone. Effects that do not target need no such permission."],
			["Badger and graveyard targets", "Rysorian Badger may target up to two creature cards in the defending player's graveyard when it attacks unblocked. Exiling them gains one life per card actually exiled, but the Badger then assigns no combat damage that turn. You can choose zero targets or decline the exchange."],
			["Ownership and the digital adaptation", "Timmerian Fiends lets the artifact's owner ante the top card of their library instead of exchanging ownership. Otherwise both cards go to their new owners' graveyards, even if the Fiends has moved elsewhere. Ownership differs from battlefield control. Digital adaptation: token copies cannot participate in this exchange; the restriction is also printed on the card and listed in the simplified-card ledger."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Alliances costs and resources", [
			["Pitch spells", "Force of Will, Pyrokinesis, Contagion, Bounty of the Hunt and Scars of the Veteran offer an alternative to their mana cost: exile another card of the required color from your hand. Force of Will and Contagion also cost one life. Choose the payment mode before targets and payment; extra taxes still apply. Their printed mana values do not become zero."],
			["Mana from your hand", "Elvish Spirit Guide can be exiled from your hand to add one green mana without using the Spell Chain. Its ability does not work from the battlefield. It cannot pay for casting itself."],
			["Library and object costs", "Some abilities exile cards from the top of your library as a cost, before anyone can respond. You need enough cards to pay. Viscerid Drone needs both a creature and a separate Swamp; its snow-Swamp ability can also destroy artifact creatures. A tap-enchanted-creature cost is not a tap-symbol ability of that creature, so summoning sickness does not stop it."],
			["Variable and repeated costs", "Gorilla Shaman pays twice X plus one to destroy a noncreature artifact whose mana value is X. Primitive Justice pays an extra one generic plus red or green for each additional artifact target; green payments also gain life. Taste of Paradise lets you pay one generic and one green repeatedly for three extra life each time."],
			["Hidden piles and linked cards", "Phyrexian Portal lets the opponent inspect ten cards and split them; the library's owner chooses which pile to exile without seeing its cards. Gustha's Scepter exiles a hand card face down and only its controller at that time may inspect it. Losing control of the Scepter or removing it sends its linked cards to their owners' graveyards."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Alliances combat and adaptations", [
			["Regeneration rewards", "Spiny Starfish counts actual regenerations this turn, not unused shields, and creates that many tokens at the end step. Soldevi Sentry gives its chosen opponent the option to draw only when its shield actually regenerates it."],
			["Prevention and redirection", "Scars of the Veteran prevents up to seven damage; a protected creature later gets one +0/+1 counter for each damage that particular shield prevented. Martyrdom grants a creature you control a repeatable one-point redirection ability for the turn. Only you may activate it, even if the creature changes control. Redirected damage retains its original source; protection and prevention apply to its new recipient."],
			["Upkeep risk", "Thought Lash exiles increasingly many library cards for cumulative upkeep. If you do not pay, it is sacrificed and a separate trigger exiles the rest of your library. Rogue Skycaptain's unpaid wages instead clear its wage counters and pass it to the opponent. Phantasmal Sphere leaves an opposing flying Orb sized by its last counter count."],
			["Extra land plays", "Storm Cauldron grants each player one extra land play each turn. A land tapped for mana returns to its owner's hand through a trigger; the mana is available before that trigger resolves. Lands that require an entry sacrifice demand payment before entering, so a failed payment creates no entry trigger."],
			["Alliances digital adaptations", "Fatal Lore, Library of Lat-Nam and Misfortune let the opponent choose their mode on resolution rather than announcement. Fatal Lore's creature targets are also chosen then, respecting normal targeting restrictions. These differences are printed on the cards and recorded in the simplified-card ledger."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Mirage block phasing and flanking", [
			["Phasing", "A permanent with phasing phases out during its controller's untap step, before anything untaps, and phases back in during that player's next untap step. While phased out it is treated as though it does not exist: it cannot be targeted, attack, block, tap for mana or be destroyed, damaged or counted, and it leaves combat. It keeps its counters, its tapped state and its place in play, and Auras on it phase out and back in with it, still attached. Phasing is not leaving or entering the battlefield, so no enters or leaves abilities trigger. Reality Ripple and similar cards phase a permanent out once; it comes back at its controller's next untap step. Nothing phases during a skipped untap step."],
			["What phasing does not change", "A phased-out permanent keeps its controller, its owner and how long it has been on the battlefield, so a creature that phases back in during your untap step can attack that turn if it could before it phased out. Tokens stay tokens. An effect that lasts for as long as the permanent stays somewhere ends when it phases out, and a spell or ability that would affect it while it is phased out does nothing to it."],
			["Phased-out cards on the table", "A phased-out permanent stays on its controller's side of the table, faded and lettered Phased out, for both players to see. Hold the pointer over it to read when it phases back in, or which card holds it out, such as Oubliette, which keeps a creature phased out until Oubliette leaves the battlefield. Clicking it does nothing. Spatial Binding and Ertai's Familiar can stop a permanent phasing out until your next upkeep; such a card says Can't phase out."],
			["Flanking", "Whenever a creature without flanking blocks a creature with flanking, the blocker gets -1/-1 until end of turn. This is a triggered ability on the Spell Chain, so the defender may answer it, for example by pumping the blocker. A blocker it kills dies before combat damage, and the attacker stays blocked; with trample all its damage goes to the player. Each instance triggers separately: Agility on a knight that already has flanking makes two triggers. A creature that loses flanking until end of turn loses every instance."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Mirage block flash and costs", [
			["Flash", "A spell with flash can be cast any time you could cast an instant, such as during combat or on your opponent's turn. Several Mirage block cards say you may cast them as though they had flash; if you do so at a time a sorcery could not have been cast, the permanent is sacrificed at the beginning of the next cleanup step, so it lasts for the rest of that turn. Cast in your own main phase with an empty Spell Chain, it stays. Winding Canyons lets you cast creature spells as though they had flash for the rest of the turn. Your hand lights these cards whenever they can be cast, and the duel stops at the fast-effect windows when you could cast one."],
			["The cleanup response window", "The cleanup step usually ends the turn without anyone acting. If a triggered ability begins there, such as a flash-cast Aura's sacrifice or Bounty of the Hunt removing its counters, or a creature dies there, the active player and then the opponent receive priority and the Spell Chain resolves as usual; then another cleanup step follows. The Phase Bar lights its Discard Phase icon meanwhile. With nothing to answer with, the duel passes this window for you."],
			["Cumulative upkeep paid in other ways", "Some cumulative upkeep costs are not mana. Psychic Vortex makes you draw a card for each age counter; Aboroth puts a -1/-1 counter on itself for each. You make the whole payment at once or sacrifice the permanent. When a player does not pay Heart of Bogardan's own cumulative upkeep, its ability triggers; the Heart is sacrificed, and the damage still counts the age counters it had. Other permanents' unpaid upkeep does not trigger it."],
			["Alternative costs and paying with objects", "Fireblast lets you sacrifice two Mountains instead of paying its mana cost, and Spinning Darkness lets you exile the top three black cards of your graveyard; choose the payment when you cast the spell. Other costs return a permanent you control to its owner's hand, exile a card from your hand, or exile the top card of your graveyard, which is always the topmost one that qualifies. Infernal Harvest, Haunting Misery and Firestorm ask for X first; X then counts the objects you give up and costs no mana. You choose the objects as you cast and may still cancel before anything is paid."],
			["Blocking for life", "Heat Wave lets nonblue creatures block your creatures only if their controller pays 1 life for each blocking creature, and blue creatures cannot block them at all. The life is paid when blockers are declared. While you choose blockers the Situation Bar shows what they will cost, and a block you could not pay for is refused."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Mirage block digital adaptations", [
			["Regenerating this way", "Debt of Loyalty and Matopi Golem add a consequence when the creature regenerates through them. If the creature also has another regeneration shield, the game uses the plain shield first without asking, and the consequence happens only when their own shield is the one used. That order is always the better one for the creature's controller."],
			["Mind Bend", "Mind Bend works like Magical Hack and Sleight of Mind: it changes one color word or one basic land type word, chosen from a short list, on a permanent that has such a word, such as a protection color, a landwalk, a land type or a basic land's mana. It cannot rewrite other rules text."],
			["Prevention bonuses hit by hit", "Shadowbane gains you life, and Honorable Passage deals damage, separately for each creature or player whose damage the shield prevented, rather than once for the whole event. The total is the same; only an effect that counts each life gain could tell the difference."],
			["Phasing out together", "When several permanents phase out at the same moment, each one notices its own phasing out but not the others', and a permanent phasing in at that moment notices them. Every card in the game that reacts to phasing reacts only to itself, so play is not affected."],
			["Two ways not to die", "If a creature with its own replacement for dying, such as Gravebane Zombie going to the top of its owner's library, would die while Forbidden Crypt is exiling cards headed to your graveyard, the creature's own replacement is applied without asking. That is the order that keeps the card."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Tempest block shadow and blocking", [
			["Shadow", "A creature with shadow can block or be blocked only by creatures with shadow. Shadow creatures slip past ordinary blockers, but they cannot block ordinary attackers either. Shadow adds to other evasion: a flying shadow creature can be blocked only by a creature that has shadow and also flying or reach. Blocks are checked only as they are declared, so gaining or losing shadow afterwards does not undo a block. Shadow Rift and Dauthi Embrace give a creature shadow until end of turn; Reality Anchor takes it away."],
			["Blocking creatures with shadow", "Heartwood Dryad and Wall of Diffusion can block creatures with shadow as though they had shadow. They do not have shadow: they still block ordinary attackers, and cards that count or affect creatures with shadow, such as Shadowstorm, pass them by. A creature that is given shadow, by contrast, can block shadow attackers but no longer ordinary ones."],
			["Creatures that must block", "Watchdog blocks each combat if able. Provoke untaps an opposing creature, which must then block this turn if able. Invasion Plans makes every creature block each combat if able and lets the attacking player choose all the blocks. Such a creature must block when it is untapped and there is an attacker it could legally block without any extra cost; its controller still chooses which attacker, unless Invasion Plans hands that choice to the attacking player. Nobody is ever forced to pay to block. A set of blocks that leaves out a creature that could have blocked is refused, and the game names that creature."],
			["Magnetic Web", "Magnetic Web puts magnet counters on creatures, yours or your opponent's, and the counters stay. If a creature with a magnet counter attacks, every other creature with a magnet counter that is able to attack must attack too, so attack with all of your able magnetized creatures or with none of them. When one attacks, a triggered ability also makes every creature with a magnet counter block that attacker this turn if able."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Tempest block buyback and casting", [
			["Buyback", "Buyback is an extra cost you may pay as you cast the spell: the game offers the normal payment and the payment with buyback. If the spell resolves, the card returns to your hand instead of your graveyard, ready to cast again. If it is countered, or does nothing because all its targets have become illegal, it goes to the graveyard and the buyback is wasted. Some buyback costs are not mana: Constant Mists sacrifices a land, Forbid discards two cards, Slaughter costs 4 life and Flowstone Flood costs 3 life and a card discarded at random. For a spell with X, such as Fanning the Flames, choose X as usual and pay the buyback on top. Buyback never changes the card's mana value, and a copy of the spell never returns to a hand."],
			["Memory Crystal", "Memory Crystal makes every player's buyback costs two generic mana cheaper. It reduces only the generic mana of the buyback itself, never below zero: never the spell's own cost, never a colored mana symbol, and nothing for a buyback paid with a land, cards or life. Two Crystals take off four. With the 1997 rules a tapped Crystal does nothing, unless it has become a creature: a tapped artifact creature keeps working."],
			["Casting without paying the mana cost", "Dream Halls lets any player cast a spell by discarding another card that shares a color with it instead of paying its mana cost; a colorless spell cannot be cast this way. Aluren lets any player cast a creature spell with mana value 3 or less without paying its mana cost. Choose the free way when you cast the spell. X counts as zero, extra costs such as buyback must still be paid, and only one way of replacing a mana cost can be used at a time. A spell cast through Dream Halls keeps its usual timing: a sorcery still waits for your main phase."],
			["Casting as though it had flash", "Rootwater Shaman lets its controller cast Aura spells with enchant creature any time they could cast an instant. Aluren gives its free creatures the same timing, but only when they are cast for free through Aluren: paying the mana cost of such a creature still needs the usual timing. Unlike the Mirage block's flash cards, nothing cast this way is sacrificed later."],
			["Mox Diamond", "Whenever Mox Diamond would enter the battlefield, you may discard a land card from your hand. If you do, the Mox enters; if you decline, or have no land card, it goes to your graveyard without ever entering, so nothing notices it arrive. Discarding the land is not playing one, so your land for the turn is still available. The Mox taps for one mana of any color."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Tempest block licids, Slivers and Spikes", [
			["Licids", "A licid is a creature with an ability that costs mana and tapping it. The licid then stops being a creature and becomes an Aura attached to the target creature, yours or an opponent's, and the rest of its text works on that creature: Gliding Licid gives flying, Calming Licid stops it attacking, Dominating Licid gives you control of it. Like any tap ability, it needs a licid that has been under your control since your turn began. If the target is no longer legal when the ability resolves, the licid stays a tapped creature. As an Aura it stays tapped, and if the enchanted creature leaves the battlefield, stops being a creature or gains protection from the licid's color, the licid goes to its owner's graveyard, as any Aura would."],
			["Ending a licid's effect", "While a licid is an Aura, the licid's controller may pay the end cost printed on it, such as one red mana for Enraging Licid, whenever they could cast an instant. Paying does not use the Spell Chain and cannot be answered. The licid becomes a creature again where it stands, unattached and still tapped, and it has its licid ability back. Right-click the licid, or your territory, to pay."],
			["Volrath's Curse", "The creature enchanted by Volrath's Curse cannot attack or block, and its activated abilities, mana abilities included, cannot be activated. That creature's controller may sacrifice any permanent they control to ignore the Curse until end of turn, whenever they could cast an instant; the sacrifice does not use the Spell Chain. Right-click the enchanted creature, or your territory, to do it. The Curse's controller may pay one generic and one blue mana to return it to its owner's hand."],
			["Slivers", "Every Sliver gives its ability to every Sliver on the battlefield: your Slivers and your opponent's, itself included. A Sliver that arrives later gets the abilities at once, and they end when the Sliver giving them leaves. A card that names Sliver creatures reaches only Slivers that are creatures; one that names Slivers reaches every permanent with that type. Bonuses add up, so two Muscle Slivers give every Sliver +2/+2. A shared activated ability, such as Clot Sliver's regeneration, belongs to each Sliver: that Sliver's controller pays for it, and it affects only that Sliver. Sliver Queen's tokens are Slivers too."],
			["Spikes", "A Spike is a 0/0 that enters with +1/+1 counters. Its ability costs two generic mana and removing one of its +1/+1 counters, and puts a +1/+1 counter on target creature: another of yours, an opponent's, or the Spike itself. The counter comes off as you activate, before anyone can respond, so a Spike that gives away its last counter dies at once, but the counter it paid for still arrives. Spike Feeder, Spike Weaver and others have further uses for their counters. With the modern rules a +1/+1 and a -1/-1 counter on one creature remove each other; with the 1997 rules both stay and add up."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Tempest block notable cards", [
			["Humility", "Humility makes every creature a 1/1 that has lost all its abilities, lands and artifacts that have become creatures included. As on the printed card, effects apply in the order they began: an ability given to a creature after Humility arrived, such as by a Flight attached later or a Jump cast this turn, still works, while one given earlier is removed. A creature's own printed abilities stay off however late it entered. Counters and bonuses still add to the 1/1, so a creature with a +1/+1 counter is 2/2. A size set before Humility arrived gives way to its 1/1, while one set afterwards wins: a Mishra's Factory animated after Humility is a 2/2, though still without abilities."],
			["Volrath's Shapeshifter", "As long as the top card of your graveyard is a creature card, Volrath's Shapeshifter has that card's full text: its name, cost, color, types, abilities, power and toughness, plus the Shapeshifter's own ability to pay two generic mana and discard a card. It changes the moment the top card changes and is itself again when the top card is not a creature. It stays the same permanent: counters, damage and tapping carry over, and no enters ability triggers. If its new toughness cannot bear the damage already marked on it, it dies."],
			["Can't be countered, and Ertai's Meddling", "Scragnoth can't be countered: a counterspell aimed at it does nothing to it. A spell that counters unless its controller pays still asks, and its other effects still happen, so Power Sink still taps the lands of a player who does not pay. Ertai's Meddling does not counter: it exiles target spell with X delay counters, and X can't be 0. At the beginning of each of that spell's controller's upkeeps one counter comes off, and when none are left the card returns to the Spell Chain as the same spell, with the same targets and choices. It is not cast again, and it can be answered like any spell. A target that has changed zones meanwhile is gone. It works on a spell that can't be countered too, and a copy of a spell exiled this way simply ceases to exist."],
			["Static Orb", "While Static Orb is untapped, no player untaps more than two permanents during their untap step; each player chooses which ones when the untap step asks. With Winter Orb also out, at most one of the two may be a land. While the Orb itself is tapped it does nothing."],
			["The Oaths", "The five Exodus Oaths help whoever is behind, and they trigger at the beginning of every player's upkeep, yours and your opponent's. If that player's opponent controls more creatures (Oath of Druids), has fewer creature cards in the graveyard (Oath of Ghouls), controls more lands (Oath of Lieges), has more life (Oath of Mages) or has more cards in hand (Oath of Scholars), the player whose upkeep it is may take the benefit: Druids reveals cards from the top of the library until a creature card, which goes onto the battlefield while the rest go to the graveyard; Ghouls returns a creature card from the graveyard to the hand; Lieges puts a basic land from the library onto the battlefield; Mages deals 1 damage to that opponent; Scholars discards the hand and draws three cards. If the opponent no longer qualifies when the ability resolves, nothing happens."],
			["Pandemonium", "Pandemonium triggers for every creature that enters the battlefield, either player's, tokens included. The entering creature's controller, not Pandemonium's, chooses any target and decides whether the damage is dealt. The creature deals damage equal to its power as the ability resolves, or its last power if it has left by then."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Tempest block digital adaptations", [
			["Living Death", "Living Death returns every exiled creature at the same moment, both players' together, so a returned Soul Warden sees all the others arrive."],
			["Skeleton Scavengers", "When Skeleton Scavengers has a regeneration shield from its own ability and another one too, the game uses its own shield first without asking, so it always gets its +1/+1 counter, even though that makes its next regeneration cost one more. Debt of Loyalty and Matopi Golem work the other way round, because their consequence is a loss."],
			["Licids and the order of effects", "A licid that becomes an Aura keeps its place in the order of effects from when it entered the battlefield, instead of taking a new place as it attaches. This matters only if another effect took the same ability away in between: if Radjan Spirit made a creature lose flying after Gliding Licid entered, attaching the Licid to it later that turn does not give flying back. Humility never meets this case, because a licid cannot use its ability while Humility is on the battlefield."],
			["Whim of Volrath", "Whim of Volrath works like Mind Bend, but only until end of turn: it changes one color word or one basic land type word, chosen from a short list, on a permanent that has such a word, such as a protection color, a landwalk, a land type or a basic land's mana. It cannot rewrite other rules text."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Urza block echo and cycling", [
			["Echo", "At the beginning of your upkeep, if a permanent with echo came under your control since your last upkeep began, you pay its echo cost or sacrifice it. You are asked once, at your first upkeep after you cast it or gained control of it; after that it stays for free. If you cannot pay, you are not asked and the permanent is sacrificed, which regeneration cannot stop. The game pays from mana in your pool first, then taps your mana sources for the rest."],
			["Echo after a change", "A permanent that leaves the battlefield and comes back is a new permanent and owes echo again, and so does one that changes controllers: its new controller pays at their next upkeep, and its old controller owes nothing. Phasing out and back in does not count. A Clone that enters as a copy of a permanent with echo has echo too and owes it, like any permanent that has just arrived; a permanent that becomes a copy later, as Vesuvan Doppelganger can at the beginning of your upkeep, has echo but owes nothing. A creature that has lost its abilities, for example under Humility, has no echo, so nothing is asked while that lasts."],
			["Cycling", "Cycling works from your hand: pay the cycling cost and discard the card to draw a card. Click the card in your hand, or right-click it, and choose Cycling. You may cycle any time you could cast an instant, even when the card itself could not be cast now. Cycling uses the Spell Chain and can be answered, but the card is discarded as you pay, so it stays in your graveyard whatever happens to the draw. A card on the battlefield cannot be cycled. Cycling is a discard, so cards that react whenever a player discards, such as Megrim, react to it."],
			["Cycling lands and Fluctuator", "Blasted Landscape, Drifting Meadow, Polluted Mire, Remote Isle, Slippery Karst and Smoldering Crater are lands with cycling. Click one in your hand and choose: play it as your land for the turn, or cycle it. Cycling it does not use up your land play, and once it is on the battlefield it is an ordinary land. Fluctuator makes the cycling abilities its controller activates cost two generic mana less, but never less than nothing, so with Fluctuator cycling usually costs nothing. It changes no other cost."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Urza block enchantments that become creatures", [
			["Opal, Veiled and Hidden enchantments", "These enchantments wait for the moment printed on them: an opponent casting a creature spell for most Opals, any spell for the Veiled ones, a particular kind of spell, land or creature for the Hidden ones, a player low on life or with an empty hand for others. Then, if the permanent is still an enchantment, it becomes the creature printed on it, such as Opal Caryatid's 2/2 Soldier, and stops being an enchantment for the rest of the game: an Aura that enchants enchantments falls off, and Disenchant can no longer target it, while anything that hits creatures can. It has been on the battlefield all along, so it can attack if you have controlled it since your turn began. Opal Acrolith can become an enchantment again for nothing, for instance in answer to a spell that would destroy it as a creature, and Hidden Stag becomes a creature when an opponent plays a land and an enchantment again when you do."],
			["Lurking Evil", "Pay half your life, rounded up, and Lurking Evil becomes a 4/4 flying Phyrexian Horror creature: at 7 life you pay 4, at 1 life your last 1. Lurking Jackals waits for an opponent to have 10 or less life, and Lurking Skirge for a creature to be put into an opponent's graveyard from the battlefield; like the Opals, each becomes its creature only while it is still an enchantment."],
			["Opalescence", "Opalescence makes each other enchantment that is not an Aura a creature as well, yours and your opponent's, with power and toughness each equal to its mana value: Glorious Anthem is a 3/3, and its own +1/+1 makes it a 4/4. It stays an enchantment too, so Disenchant and Wrath of God both destroy it, and an enchantment with mana value 0 is a 0/0 and dies. Two Opalescences make each other creatures. An Opal enchantment under Opalescence is a creature of its mana value while it waits, and still triggers, because it is still an enchantment; once it becomes its printed creature it is no longer an enchantment, and Opalescence leaves it alone."],
			["Opalescence and Humility", "Under Opalescence, Humility is a creature too, a 4/4 by its mana value, so it takes away its own ability along with every other creature's; its effect goes on all the same. Which arrived first decides the sizes. Humility first, then Opalescence: Humility and the other enchantments are as big as their mana values, every other creature is a 1/1, and none of them has abilities. Opalescence first, then Humility: every creature is a 1/1 with no abilities, Humility and the enchantments included. Even two Opalescences with Humility kill nothing: each still sizes the enchantments after losing its own abilities."],
			["Soul Sculptor", "Soul Sculptor pays {1}{W} and taps to make target creature an enchantment that is no longer a creature and has lost all its abilities, until a player casts a creature spell. A creature spell cast by either player ends it at once, before that spell resolves, and the effect lasts even if Soul Sculptor leaves the battlefield. An Opal that has become a creature can be turned back into an enchantment this way; when the effect ends it is its printed creature again. Under Opalescence a sculpted card is a creature the size of its mana value, still without abilities."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Urza block untapping and mana", [
			["Untap up to N lands", "Great Whale, Peregrine Drake, Palinchron, Cloud of Faeries and Treachery untap up to a number of lands when they enter the battlefield; Snap, Frantic Search, Rewind and Time Spiral untap them as they resolve. Untapping the lands that paid for the spell gives that mana back, so such a card can end up costing little or nothing. The lands untap even if the creature has left the battlefield by then. It is not an untap step, so Winter Orb and Static Orb do not limit it. Rewind untaps nothing if the spell it aims at is gone, since it then does nothing at all."],
			["Choosing the lands", "These cards target nothing: you choose as the spell or ability resolves. First choose how many lands, from none up to the number printed; then choose that many lands, one at a time. Only tapped lands are offered, yours first and those that make the most mana first, such as Tolarian Academy before an Island. A tapped land of your opponent's may be chosen too. With no tapped land anywhere, nothing is asked."],
			["Untapping for a price", "Grim Monolith and Phyrexian Colossus do not untap during your untap step: pay {4} to untap the Monolith, or 8 life to untap the Colossus. Horseshoe Crab, Morphling, Vigilant Drake and Blizzard Elemental pay mana to untap themselves; that ability has no tap symbol, so it works even on the turn the creature arrives. Voltaic Key pays {1} and taps itself to untap target artifact. Such abilities can be used again and again while you can pay; a combination that would never end, such as Palinchron with High Tide, is stopped by the game's loop limit (Which rules this game plays by, Endless loops)."],
			["Lands that count", "Tolarian Academy makes one blue mana for each artifact you control, Gaea's Cradle one green mana for each creature and Serra's Sanctum one white mana for each enchantment, counted as you tap the land; with none, it makes nothing. They count what your permanents are now: an animated land is a creature, an artifact creature counts for both the Academy and the Cradle, and an Opal enchantment that has become a creature counts for the Cradle but no longer for the Sanctum. Automatic mana payment counts them too. They are legendary lands, so a second one of the same name goes to the graveyard."],
			["Storage Matrix", "While Storage Matrix is untapped, each player chooses artifacts, creatures or lands in their untap step, and only their permanents of that kind untap; an artifact land or an artifact creature counts as either of its kinds. The duel asks you at the start of your untap step, unless nothing of yours is tapped. Winter Orb and Static Orb then limit what is left. A tapped Storage Matrix restricts nothing."],
			["Thran Turbine and Contamination", "At the beginning of your upkeep Thran Turbine may add {C}{C} that cannot be spent to cast spells: it pays for abilities, echo, cumulative upkeep and other costs that are not spells, and it empties, and burns under mana burn, like any other mana. While Contamination is on the battlefield, every land tapped for mana makes exactly one black mana instead of anything else, so Gaea's Cradle and Tolarian Academy make one black mana too; Mana Flare still adds its extra mana. At the beginning of your upkeep Contamination asks you to sacrifice a creature, or it is sacrificed itself."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Urza block counters to cash in", [
			["Verse counters", "Serra's Liturgy, Lilting Refrain, Recantation, Discordant Dirge, Vile Requiem, Rumbling Crescendo, Torch Song, Midsummer Revel and War Dance ask at the beginning of each of your upkeeps whether to put a verse counter on them. Later you sacrifice the enchantment, with any other cost printed, for an effect as big as its verse counters. When it says up to X targets, X is counted as you choose the targets, before the sacrifice; any other X is the number of verse counters it had as it was sacrificed. Either way, a counter added in response changes nothing."],
			["Serra's Hymn", "Serra's Hymn gathers verse counters the same way. Sacrifice it to prevent the next X damage that would be dealt this turn, X being its verse counters, divided as you choose among any number of creatures and players, with at most one target for each point. Click a target once for each point it should get. The shares are fixed as you activate it; a target that is gone by then takes its share with it. With no verse counter there is nothing to prevent."],
			["Counters that pay out", "Barrin's Codex, Lotus Blossom, Powder Keg and Smokestack ask at your upkeep whether to add a page, petal, fuse or soot counter. Barrin's Codex draws a card for each page counter and Lotus Blossom makes one mana of a color of your choice for each petal counter, both counted as you sacrifice them; Powder Keg destroys each artifact and creature whose mana value equals its fuse counters; Smokestack makes each player, at the beginning of their upkeep, sacrifice a permanent for each soot counter. Momentum, Private Research, Incendiary and Festering Wound are Auras that gather counters the same way."],
			["Archery Training", "At the beginning of your upkeep you may put an arrow counter on Archery Training. The creature it enchants can tap to deal X damage to target attacking or blocking creature, where X is the number of arrow counters on that Training. If the Training leaves the battlefield in answer, the shot still deals the number it had as the creature tapped. Two Trainings on one creature give it two separate abilities, each counting its own arrows."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Urza block cards put onto the battlefield", [
			["Arriving together", "Show and Tell, Replenish, Exhume, Victimize, Planar Birth, Defense of the Heart and Living Death put several cards onto the battlefield at one moment. Every choice is made first, the active player's before the opponent's; then all the cards arrive together, so a card that reacts whenever another creature enters, such as Soul Warden, sees every one of the others. Cards put onto the battlefield are not cast: no counterspell can stop them, abilities that trigger when a spell is cast do not trigger, and a land put there this way is not your land for the turn."],
			["Show and Tell", "Each player may put an artifact, creature, enchantment or land card from their hand onto the battlefield. The active player chooses first, then the opponent, without seeing that choice: neither card is shown until both players have chosen, then both are revealed and arrive together, each under its owner's control."],
			["Auras put onto the battlefield", "An Aura put onto the battlefield without being cast, by Replenish, Show and Tell or Academy Rector, targets nothing: its controller chooses what it enchants among the permanents already there, never one arriving with it, and protection still keeps it off. An Aura with nothing it could enchant stays where it was: Replenish leaves it in your graveyard, and Show and Tell and Academy Rector offer only an Aura that has something to enchant."],
			["Sneak Attack and other cards put into play", "Sneak Attack's ability costs {R} and can be used any time you could cast an instant: put a creature card from your hand onto the battlefield. It gains haste, so it can attack at once, and it is sacrificed at the beginning of the next end step if you still control it. Copper Gnomes and Quicksilver Amulet put an artifact or a creature card from your hand onto the battlefield, Tinker an artifact card from your library, and Academy Rector, when it dies, lets you exile it to put an enchantment card from your library onto the battlefield. None of these cards is cast, but what a card does as it enters still happens: a Phyrexian Processor still asks for its life."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Urza block other zones and revealed cards", [
			["Yawgmoth's Will", "Until end of turn you may play lands and cast spells from your graveyard. A land played from there is your land for the turn; a spell keeps its usual timing and costs. For the rest of the turn every card that would be put into your graveyard is exiled instead: Yawgmoth's Will itself, the spells you cast again, the cards you discard and your creatures that die. To play a card from your graveyard, click your graveyard: the cards you may play now are ringed, their names yellow; click one."],
			["Temporal Aperture", "Pay {5} and tap Temporal Aperture: your library is shuffled and its top card is revealed to both players. Until end of turn, as long as that card stays on top, you may play it without paying its mana cost: a land as your land for the turn, a spell at the time it could normally be cast, with X counting as zero. Drawing it, putting another card on top or any shuffle of your library ends the offer for good, even if the card is on top again afterwards. Click your library to play the card; a spell then offers to be cast without paying its mana cost."],
			["Revealing cards from your hand", "The Seers and the Scents of Urza's Destiny, and Rofellos's Gift, reveal any number of cards of their color from your hand as they resolve: choose how many, then which ones. The revealed cards stay in your hand, both players see them, and each one adds to the effect: 2 life each for Scent of Jasmine, one more mana to pay for Scent of Brine, one enchantment card back from your graveyard for Rofellos's Gift. Revealing none is allowed. Metalworker does the same as it taps for mana: {C}{C} for each artifact card revealed from your hand. You choose how many, then which ones unless you reveal them all; automatic mana payment counts every artifact card in your hand."],
			["Abundance", "Abundance replaces each of your draws with a question: draw the card, or reveal cards from the top of your library until a land, or until a nonland card. The card found goes to your hand, and the other revealed cards go to the bottom of your library in the order you choose; if none of that kind is left, every revealed card goes to the bottom and you draw nothing. The duel stops in your draw step to ask you too."],
			["Memory Jar", "Tap and sacrifice Memory Jar: each player exiles the cards in their hand face down, where nobody may look at them, and draws seven cards. At the beginning of the next end step each player discards their hand and takes back the cards exiled this way. Used during an end step, Memory Jar gives the exiled cards back at the next turn's end step."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Urza block graveyards and counterspells", [
			["Put into a graveyard from anywhere", "Some Urza block cards notice every card that reaches a graveyard, whatever it came from: a spell that resolved or was countered, a discarded or milled card, or a permanent that died. Planar Void exiles each other card put into any graveyard. Energy Field prevents all damage dealt to you by sources you don't control, but it is sacrificed when a card is put into your graveyard, even your own spell as it resolves. Compost may draw you a card whenever a black card is put into an opponent's graveyard. Tokens are not cards, so they never count, and a card exiled instead, by Yawgmoth's Will or Forbidden Crypt, never reached a graveyard."],
			["Serra Avatar", "Serra Avatar's power and toughness are each equal to your life total and change the moment your life does: if your life falls to the damage already marked on it, it dies at once, even in the middle of combat. Whenever it is put into a graveyard from anywhere, from the battlefield, your hand, your library or the Spell Chain, it is shuffled back into its owner's library, even when another player controlled it."],
			["Multani's Presence", "Whenever a spell you cast is countered, Multani's Presence draws you a card, also when you do not pay for a spell that counters unless you pay, such as Force Spike. A spell that does nothing because all its targets have become illegal was not countered, and a copy of a spell was never cast: neither draws a card."],
			["Auras that come back", "Rancor, Brilliant Halo, Launch, Despondency, Fiery Mantle, Fortitude, Spreading Algae, Cessation, Slow Motion, Sleeper's Guile and Sluggishness return to their owner's hand when they are put into a graveyard from the battlefield: when they are destroyed, and when what they enchant dies or leaves and they fall off. Cast it again and it comes back again. An Aura exiled instead, by Forbidden Crypt or Yawgmoth's Will, stays exiled."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Urza block combat", [
			["Defensive Formation", "Defensive Formation lets its controller, rather than the attacking player, assign the combat damage of each creature attacking them, divided as they choose among the creatures blocking it: a gang block can share out the damage so that fewer blockers die. With trample, the defender places the extra damage too and may keep it all on the blockers. An unblocked attacker is not affected, and the attacking player's own Defensive Formation does nothing."],
			["Okk", "Okk can attack only if a creature with greater power attacks too, and block only if a creature with greater power also blocks. Power is compared as attackers or blockers are declared; a later boost changes nothing. While no such companion is possible, Okk is not offered as an attacker or blocker. Under Humility it has no abilities and so no restriction."],
			["Outmaneuver", "Outmaneuver targets X blocked creatures, so cast it after blockers are declared. Each of them deals all its combat damage to the player it attacks, as though it were not blocked. It still counts as blocked, and its blockers still deal their damage to it. Thorn Elemental can do the same by itself when its controller chooses to; Outmaneuver's creatures do it without being asked."],
			["Blocking many, and needing many blockers", "Wall of Glare can block any number of creatures: while choosing blockers, click the Wall, then each attacker it should block. Phyrexian Colossus, on the other hand, cannot be blocked except by three or more creatures."],
		]),
		_page("Abilities — Urza block notable cards", [
			["Gilded Drake", "When Gilded Drake enters the battlefield, you may choose a creature an opponent controls: the two creatures then change controllers for good, the Drake going to your opponent. If you choose none, or that creature is gone or cannot change controllers by the time the ability resolves, the Drake is sacrificed instead. If the Drake is no longer yours by then, nothing happens."],
			["Donate and Treachery", "Donate gives target player control of target permanent you control, for good. Treachery is an Aura: you control the creature it enchants for as long as it stays attached, and when Treachery enters the battlefield it untaps up to five lands, so it can pay for itself."],
			["Masticore", "At the beginning of your upkeep you discard a card or sacrifice Masticore; with an empty hand it is sacrificed. The discard is a payment, yet a card that reacts to discards, such as Megrim, still sees it. Masticore pays {2} to deal 1 damage to target creature and {2} to regenerate, as often as you can pay."],
			["Phyrexian Processor", "As Phyrexian Processor enters the battlefield, choose how much life to pay. Then {4} and a tap make an X/X black Phyrexian Minion token, X being the life paid; if the Processor is destroyed in answer, the Minion still comes at full size. Paying no life makes 0/0 Minions, which die at once."],
			["Thran Weaponry", "Thran Weaponry gives +2/+2 to the creatures that are on the battlefield, yours and your opponent's, when its ability resolves. A creature that arrives later gets nothing. The bonus ends for good the moment the Weaponry untaps or leaves the battlefield; tapping it again does not bring it back. You may keep it tapped through your untap step to keep the bonus."],
			["Defense Grid and Thran Lens", "Defense Grid makes every spell cost {3} more except during its controller's own turn, for both players: an answer cast on the other player's turn costs more. Abilities are not spells and cost no more. Thran Lens makes every permanent colorless, while spells and cards in hands and graveyards keep their colors: a black creature under the Lens is nonblack, so Terror can destroy it. With the 1997 rules a tapped Grid or Lens does nothing, unless it has become a creature: a tapped artifact creature keeps working."],
			["Damping Engine", "Damping Engine stops the player who controls more permanents than the other from playing lands and casting artifact, creature and enchantment spells; with equal counts it stops nobody, and the permanents are counted again at every play. That player may sacrifice a permanent they control, Damping Engine itself if it is theirs, to ignore it until end of turn: right-click your territory, or Damping Engine, to do it. With the 1997 rules a tapped Damping Engine does nothing."],
		]),
	]

static func _page(title: String, entries: Array) -> Dictionary:
	var blocks: Array = []
	for entry in entries:
		blocks.append({"kind": "heading", "text": entry[0]})
		blocks.append({"kind": "text", "text": entry[1]})
	return {"title": title, "blocks": blocks}
